        <p>
          Against the <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-2450-treadmill">Commercial 2450</a>,
          the case for spending more comes down to two things: a
          meaningfully larger touchscreen and a motor with more reserve
          capacity for sustained hard running. Everything else — deck,
          incline range, cushioning — is identical, which is exactly why we
          think the 2450 is the smarter buy for the large majority of
          shoppers. Against the <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill">Commercial 1750</a>,
          the gap is bigger and easier to justify if you're a serious
          runner: a stronger motor, a steeper incline ceiling, and a
          dramatically larger screen, all for roughly $2,200 more.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you're weighing the 2950 against machines outside NordicTrack's
          own lineup entirely, it's worth browsing our full
          <a href="/commercial-treadmills">commercial treadmill roundup</a>,
          where flagship machines from other brands land in a similar price
          range with different trade-offs — some prioritize even larger
          running surfaces over screen size, which may matter more to you
          than it does to the average NordicTrack shopper.
        </p>

