              majority of shoppers.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What's the actual difference between the Commercial 2950 and the Commercial 2450?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              The 2950 steps up to a 4.5 CHP motor from the 2450's 4.25 CHP,
              a modest but real difference under sustained load, and swaps
              the 2450's 24-inch touchscreen for a 32-inch display, the
              largest in NordicTrack's lineup. Incline range, deck size, and
              weight capacity are identical between the two models, so the
              price gap is almost entirely about motor headroom and screen
              size.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Commercial 2950 overkill for casual walkers?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Honestly, yes. A casual walker or occasional jogger will not
              meaningfully feel the difference between this motor and the
              one in the Commercial 1750, which costs roughly $2,200 less.
              The 2950 earns its price with serious daily runners and
              households that want the largest possible screen for
              interactive iFit content, not with light or moderate users.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How does the Commercial 2950 compare to the Commercial 1750?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              The gap is substantial: a stronger 4.5 CHP motor versus 3.75
              CHP, a steeper 15% max incline versus 12%, and a 32-inch
              touchscreen versus 14 inches, all for roughly $2,200 more.
              Runners doing serious incline training or households that
              will put heavy daily mileage on the machine will feel that
              gap; casual users generally won't.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the 32-inch touchscreen actually matter during a workout?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              It's a genuinely different experience from a 14-to-24 inch
              screen, especially for iFit's trainer-led trail and studio
              workouts, where the larger display makes the video content
              feel closer to watching television than glancing at a tablet.
              Whether that's worth well over a thousand dollars depends
              entirely on how much you value that specific experience.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How loud is the Commercial 2950 at higher speeds?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              We measured noise in the low-to-mid 60 decibel range during
              sustained running, similar to the Commercial 1750 despite the
              stronger motor, thanks to a well-insulated motor housing. It
              stayed consistent through 45-minute continuous runs without
              any noticeable increase in noise as the motor warmed up.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does assembly take on the Commercial 2950?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Roughly 90 minutes with two people, longer than the Commercial
              1750 largely because of the significantly heavier 32-inch
              touchscreen mast, which the manual explicitly says should not
              be lifted by one person. Budget closer to two hours if you're
              building it entirely solo, and expect a heavier shipping
              crate than any other model in the lineup.
            </p>
